Symbol Web
A LOT ฦ think of symbols as those pesky little things that were only important in lit class. Big
ु. If instead you think of symbols as ศ sewn into the story tapestry that have great ح
effect, you'll have some idea of the power of this set of story µ.
Symbol is a ¤ of the small. It is the word or object that stands for something else—person,
place, action, or thing—and is repeated many times over the course of the story. Just as character, theme,
and plot are big ษ to fool and please the , symbol is the small puzzle that works its magic
deep below the surface. Symbols are ì to your success as a ¢ because they give you a
hidden language that حly sways the .

HOW ƭ WORK
A symbol is an image with special power that has value to the . Just as matter is ń
Ș energy, a symbol is ń Ș meaning. In fact, it is the most focused condenser-
expander of any ¦ ¤. A simple guide to using symbol might be "Refer and repeat."
Here's how it works: yon start with a ช and create a symbol that will cause that ช in the
. You then repeat the symbol, changing it slightly.
Feeling -> symbol -> ช in the 
Changed symbol -> stronger ช in the 
Symbols work on the  in a very ฬ but Ú way. A symbol creates a ฮ,
like ripples in a pond, every time it λ. As you repeat the symbol, the ripples expand and
Ӂ in the ็ of the  often without their being consciously aware of it.

三 WEB
You may recall that I said that the single ت ु in creating character is to see a
character as a single, unique individual. That's the quickest way to make sure that none of your
characters is a unique individual. Similarly, the single ت ु in creating a symbol is to
see it as a single object.
KEY POINT: Always create a web of symbols in which each symbol т define the
others.
Let's step back for a moment and look once more at how the γ subsystems of the
story body fit á. The character web shows a û truth about how the world works by
comparing and contrasting people. Plot shows a û truth about how the world works
through a sequence of actions with a surprising but Ú logic. The symbol web shows a
û ‎ about how the world works by referring objects, people, and actions to other
objects, people, and actions. When the  makes that comparison, even if partially or
fleetingly, they see the บ nature of the two things being compared.
For example, to compare Tracy Lord to a Ŧ in The Philadelphia Story 使
her ج and grace, but also her coldness and fierce sense of ャ to others. To
compare the serene forest world of  to the Д Р world of Ț in Ihe
Lord of the ا ► the contrast between a sweet, life-л community of equals and a
fiery, ฯ world of Ŕ. To compare airplanes to horses in For Whom the Bell Tolls
ึ how an ¹ culture valuing Ȝ, ル force is replacing a horse
culture valuing personal ĕ, loyalty, and honor.
You create the symbol web by attaching symbols to any or all of these elements: the ¹
story, the structure, characters, theme, story world, actions, objects, and ©.
