A scene is し one action in one time and place. It is the basic unit of what actually đ in
the story, right now, as the  أ it. The scene ô is the sequence of these units. To
be a great ¢, you must create a ô that is like a fine tapestry, picking up one thread for a
moment before letting that thread dip back under the surface before appearing again a bit later on.
The scene ô, also known as the scene list, scene outline, or scene breakdown, is the final step
before writing your full story or script. It is a list of every scene you ̀ will be in the final story, along
with a tag for any scene in which a structure step э.
The scene ô is an И П step in the writing process. Like the seven steps,
character web, and ä sequence, it is a way of seeing how the story fits á Ʊ the
surface.
The scene ô is really an extension of the plot. It is your plot in minute detail. The point of the
scene ô is to get one last look at the overall architecture of the story before writing it. Therefore,
don't go into Too much derail, because this will hide the structure. Try to describe each scene in one line.
